{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And He blotted out all existence.</b> Meaning: besides the dying alluded to in the preceding verse, also everything that stood of them was blotted out (Sanhedrin 108b) through the agency of the abundance of the waters' heat and cold, and their entire being was blotted out and they became like water and were as though they had never existed in the world. Through this we will understand the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Berakhot 61b), and this is their language: Rabbi Ami said (Psalms 119): ״אָחוֹר וָקֶדֶם צַרְתָּנִי״ — אָחוֹר (behind) for the act of Creation, and קֶדֶם (before) for punishment, as it is written ״מֵאָדָם עַד בְּהֵמָה״ and so forth, end quote. And it is difficult: is not the Scripture explicit, for the preceding verse said ״וַיִּגְוַע כָּל בָּשָׂר הָרוֹמֵשׂ״ and so forth ״וְכָל הָאָדָם״ — behold, after arranging all the created beings, it mentioned the dying of man. According to our words, this is intended to refer specifically to the form of the body, for the body was created last of all, and when HaShem blotted out all existence, the golem of his form was first, as we have written. And note as well the precision of the word ״צַרְתָּנִי״ (You formed me), which refers back to the body, as it is written ״וַיִּיצֶר ה׳״ and so forth ״עָפָר מִן הָאֲדָמָה״. But regarding the matter of dying, which is the departure of the soul from the body, it was not so, for man's soul did not depart except after everything else, as is understood from the verse."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The Talmudic passage in Berakhot 61b connects ״אָחוֹר״ to man's being created last in Creation and ״קֶדֶםש to his perishing first in the Flood; the Or HaChaim's difficulty is that the preceding verse (Genesis 7:21) seems to list man's death last, not first."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:40:12.195548203Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}